## Rate Limiting — What Happens Automatically

All Genesys Platform API calls share one request path with rate limit handling built in — **you do not need to do anything special**.

### Automatic behaviour

| Response | Action |
|---|---|
| `429 Too Many Requests` | Reads `Retry-After` header (seconds), adds ±30% random jitter, sleeps, retries — up to **4 times** |
| `5xx` **with** `Retry-After` | Same wait + retry — up to **2 times** |
| `5xx` **without** `Retry-After` | Throws immediately — Genesys docs say do not auto-retry these |
| Retries exhausted | Throws `GenesysApiError` with a clear message |

Every retry logs to stderr: `[rate-limit] 429 on GET /api/v2/... — waiting 12.3s (retry 1/4)`

### Why jitter?

Bulk operations (`fetch_transcripts_bulk`, parallel eval subagents) fire many concurrent requests. Without jitter, all workers hit 429 together and retry in lockstep — triggering a second wave. The ±30% randomisation spreads retries out so they don't pile up.

### Preview API — confirmed limits and safe batch size

The Genesys preview summary API has two relevant limits:

- **`token.rate.per.minute`: 300** — applies to all API calls for the authenticated token
- **Notification channel burst sensitivity** — each preview request creates a short-lived notification channel (`POST /api/v2/notifications/channels`). This endpoint is sensitive to concurrent bursts independent of the per-minute token budget. In testing, firing **≥ 15 concurrent** preview requests reliably triggers `429 Failed to create notification channel`; **8 concurrent requests** is safe.

**Safe `batch_size` for `prepare_prompt_test`: 8.** Do not exceed 10. The retry logic will recover from a channel 429, but it adds latency and unnecessary API pressure. Staying at batch_size=8 gives full parallel throughput without hitting the burst threshold.

### What agents should know

- **Long pauses during bulk operations are normal.** If you see `[rate-limit]` log lines, the server is honouring Genesys's `Retry-After` header — just wait, do not cancel or restart.
- **Do not reduce `concurrency` preemptively.** The retry logic handles bursts gracefully. Only reduce concurrency if you are consistently exhausting all 4 retries on 429s.
- **Token expiry during a long backoff wait is handled.** The token is re-fetched at the start of every retry attempt, so a 65-second wait will not cause auth failures.
- **`Retry-After` values from Genesys are in seconds.** A typical value is 10–30 s; the cap in code is 65 s (including jitter).
- **`prepare_prompt_test` uses `batch_size=8` by default.** Do not override it above 10 without expecting possible 429s on notification channel creation.

